+## Rework — mock-Comfy merge-queue failure
+
+The second rejection targeted `generate_ui::draft_refine::tests::draft_refine_mock_comfy_uses_source_and_library_results_can_be_used_or_opened`. Ran `git -c user.name=Claude -c user.email=noreply@anthropic.com merge --no-commit claude/sleepy-franklin-egimjb` on job-result commit `9f9f2e75`; Git reported `Already up to date.` Coordinator commit `9247cc397d929a21e9abba721ad963a4410a7d2b` is already an ancestor, so there were no new conflicts to resolve. The previously merged file-input, save, translation and gate fixes remain intact.
+
+| Failure | Re-check and real cause | Fix | Regression coverage |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Mock-Comfy Draft/Refinement integration | Reproduced using the queue binary with the normal profile: failure at 10.36 seconds. An instrumented rebuild failed at 10.34 seconds with one pending refinement job and **zero mock prompts**. Read-only inspection of that test process's open files confirmed it was reading the configured `flux-2-klein-9b-fp8.safetensors` (9,433,061,528 bytes). Refine discovery finds and hashes local weights even when HTTP points to MockComfy. A subsequent warm-cache diagnostic narrowly passed at 10.12 seconds; this was dependence on real weights and I/O timing, not a missing result expectation. | The test advertises a unique mock-only Klein 9B filename from a small temporary model fixture, discovers it through the normal inventory API and selects it before the same Refine button click. Cleanup restores the service override/catalogue and removes the temporary fixture. Added assertions that its family matches Klein 9B and the submitted UNETLoader uses that exact mock filename. Production refine discovery/hashing is unchanged. | Same HTTP refinement workflow, source LoadImage/VAEEncode assertions, saved library image, one undo step, undo and Open-as-new-document assertions. The original ten-second deadline is unchanged. |
+| Flaky full-app drag-and-drop placement | The queue's first run failed, then passed on retry. The existing test allowed the tab-open worker to finish but checked canvas placement after only two UI frames. File-input work moved placement decoding to a worker; frame count does not establish its completion. | Continue pumping frames, with a bounded 30-second wait for the transform to appear, before the original placement and Escape assertions. This matches the existing background-open deadline and does not change application behavior. | Same full-app drop service/path, tab ordering, no new document, one added layer, Place transform, and Escape cancellation/removal assertions. |
+
+All pre-existing assertions in these two tests are retained. No test was removed, renamed, ignored or weakened, and no expected behavior changed in this rework. The original pool `TEST-CHANGED:` declaration earlier in this report remains the only expectation change. The new mock fixture strengthens the existing test with two assertions. There are no process-global environment changes for test isolation: validation uses the normal profile, with neither `LOCAL_IMAGE_DATA_DIR` nor `LOCAL_IMAGE_MODELS_DIR` set, and default test parallelism. No GUI window was opened and neither Local Image nor the running ComfyUI was stopped.
+
+The first Cargo reproduction against the shared queue target directory encountered a stale `lightcraft-preview` dependency artifact (`try_recv_failure` appeared missing although present in the checked-in source). Subsequent builds use a task-owned target cache seeded via filesystem reflinks; that cache's `lightcraft-preview` artifacts were cleaned and rebuilt. The resulting reproduction compiled successfully and reproduced the actual ten-second test failure. A broader run then exposed additional copied stale artifacts: the cached codec rlib did not contain `JPEG dimensions exceed TIFF chunk`, although the current source did, and both new codec regressions failed against that old library. Interrupted only the task-owned test process tree and cleaned **all workspace packages** from the task-owned cache before final validation, retaining only third-party dependencies. These cache failures were addressed by rebuilding current source, with no production or test-expectation edits. The first broad run also hit the unchanged People-view two-second responsiveness check while workspace check/Clippy ran concurrently. It failed again in the rebuilt broad run without a concurrent validation build: the full Library UI target took 420.06 seconds. Its exact retry then passed in 0.64 seconds with the same normal profile, default test parallelism and original two-second assertion. This is an untouched timing failure under broad parallel execution; no People source, fixture or expectation was changed. Final builds and runtime tests are sequential. All final commands pin rustup Rust 1.98.1, retain `RUSTC_WRAPPER=li-rustc-gate`, use `--offline`, and add no Cargo job cap or test-profile override.
+
+Final required validation passed: the rebuilt 28-package merge-queue suite completed with **7,891 passed**, 1 failed and 124 ignored. Its sole failure was the unchanged People-view two-second timing assertion; the exact retry passed without altering its test, deadline, profile or test parallelism. All **54 original file-input regression names** were matched to individual passing lines. This includes the two codec regressions that failed against the stale library. The People responsiveness test passed its exact retry; its broader target ran for 420.06 seconds under the current machine load, compared with 149.48 seconds in the earlier diagnostic run. No People source or test was changed. The full Compositing UI library suite and both coordinator-reported drop/refinement tests passed under normal profile/default parallelism. Workspace check, strict all-targets Clippy for all 20 affected crates, formatting and `git diff --check` also passed. Changes remain uncommitted.
